          "On June 16 in Abidjan, Alassane Ouattara and John Dramani Mahama signed a declaration committing to harmonize farmgate prices, premiums and season calendars, and to open the Côte d'Ivoire-Ghana Cocoa Initiative to other producers ([CIGCI](https://www.cighci.org/presidents-of-cote-divoire-and-ghana-agree-to-harmonise-cocoa-farm-gate-pricing-policies/)). A month later, Nigeria, Ghana, Côte d'Ivoire and Cameroon adopted a declaration in Abuja aimed at ending raw bean exports ([Arise News](https://www.arise.tv/nigeria-ghana-cote-divoire-cameroon-sign-abuja-declaration-to-end-raw-cocoa-exports/)). The four signatories, which claim about 75% of world output, also undertook to negotiate with buyers as a bloc and to defend a common position on the European regulation, seeking recognition of their national traceability systems. A joint Ivorian-Ghanaian technical group met in Accra on July 28 and 29. Yet three months after Abidjan, the world's two largest producers are paying their farmers prices that differ by 75%.",
          "George Stigler explained why agreements among sellers come apart: they hold only if each party can observe the other's conduct and if deviating from the rule carries a cost. The June declaration sets an objective without a common formula. On one side, a price indexed to at least 70% of FOB; on the other, a price anchored in forward sales and in 60% of the CIF price, with a floor of 50% in periods of high volatility. Each country can apply its own rule to the letter and end up with a price far removed from its neighbour's, without ever formally breaching the agreement. The problem, then, is not cheating in Stigler's sense but the absence of a rule from which deviation would be observable: without a shared benchmark, neither country can accuse the other of breaking it.",
