          "Albert Hirschman distinguished two ways of responding to a disappointing organization: leaving it or making one's voice heard within it. In January 2025, Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger chose the first. Twenty-one months later, the process that opened in Dakar shows the limits of that option: institutional exit does not remove the need for a negotiated relationship as long as trucks, ports, herds and families keep moving. Hirschman also observed that exit is all the more costly when the departing member remains dependent on the goods the organization provides. For three landlocked countries whose imports, fuel and part of their livestock exports pass through coastal ports and markets, the West African economic space is precisely such a good, and political rupture offers no way of replacing it.",
          "On October 17, 2026, Senegal's foreign minister Cheikh Niang, chair of the ECOWAS Council of Ministers, and his Burkinabè counterpart Karamoko Jean-Marie Traoré, chair of the AES ministerial committee, co-chaired the first ministerial meeting between the two organizations since the three countries' withdrawal took effect. Their joint communiqué, published by ECOWAS on October 18, contains a single decision: to set up thematic working groups tasked with preparing a framework agreement. The day before, Mr Traoré had ruled out any return to ECOWAS, and Abuja let it be known that it expected a draft for the December summit."
