          "Oliver Williamson showed that relationship-specific investments, those that lose their value if the relationship breaks down, are made only if the counterparty's commitment is credible. A truck fleet dedicated to the Abidjan-Bamako corridor, a bonded warehouse in Lomé or a network of livestock traders between Fada N'Gourma and Kumasi are assets of this kind. A regime that can be revoked at any moment weighs on such decisions even if it is never revoked. The haulier who hesitates to renew a fleet, the bank that shortens the tenor of its transit trade loans, the port operator that postpones a terminal extension: all of them price in a political risk premium. The cost of uncertainty is thus measured in deferred investment rather than in interrupted flows, which makes it invisible in trade statistics.",
