          "The dependence is nonetheless asymmetric, and that is where Keohane and Nye locate bargaining power. A World Bank assessment from May 2024 estimated that 37% of AES imports came from ECOWAS, whereas only 1 to 2% of Malian and Burkinabè exports, and about 6% of Nigerien exports, went there, with gold leaving mostly for destinations outside the region. In the other direction, Mali is Senegal's largest customer, at about 803 billion CFA francs in 2024, more than a quarter of its exports, and about 90% of Burkina Faso's live animal exports go to Ghana and Côte d'Ivoire.",
