          "In 2017 Graham Allison popularized the idea of a “Thucydides Trap”: of sixteen historical cases in which a rising power threatened to displace an established one, twelve ended in war. The framework usefully reminds us that great-power rivalries have their own dynamics, made of fear, pride and misperception. It also has limits that the Beijing summit brings into view. Allison's sample is constructed after the fact and mixes eras in which war was an ordinary instrument of policy. It takes no account of nuclear deterrence, which makes a direct clash far costlier than in previous centuries. And it underestimates the density of today's economic ties, which do not make war impossible but raise its cost for both sides.",
