          "The summit's third consequence is diplomatic. In 2008 Kuik Cheng-Chwee defined strategic hedging as the behavior of states that, uncertain about the intentions of the great powers, combine opposing commitments so as to depend on none: economic cooperation with one, security guarantees from the other, and a refusal to choose exclusively. He was describing Southeast Asia. Most African governments now pursue the same strategy, heir to a non-alignment born at Bandung in 1955 but stripped of its ideological dimension. The aim is no longer to keep two blocs at arm's length, but to trade and negotiate with everyone.",
