          "In 1983, James Hamilton observed that all but one of the post-war US recessions had been preceded, with a lag of roughly three quarters, by a sharp rise in the price of crude. Four decades on, the link between oil and the business cycle holds in principle, but the geography of the shock has changed. West Africa now counts several crude producers, from Nigeria to Senegal by way of Ghana, Côte d'Ivoire and Niger, yet the region still imports much of the fuel it consumes. A dearer barrel there enriches treasuries while impoverishing motorists, sometimes within the same country.",
