          "In 1961 Bela Balassa described economic integration as a sequence of stages, from free trade area to economic union, each requiring states to surrender a little more of their policy autonomy. Africa has often climbed that ladder from the top down: the 1991 Abuja Treaty already set the goal of a continental economic community, while the first rung, effective free trade among the continent's economies, had been reached nowhere outside a handful of regional communities. The African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), signed in Kigali in March 2018, in force since May 2019 and open for trading on 1 January 2021, was meant at last to build that first rung across 54 countries.",
