          "The five signatories that had not ratified by mid-2025, Benin, Libya, Somalia, Sudan and South Sudan, are not all on the same path. Somalia's parliament [approved the agreement](https://dawan.africa/news/somalias-parliament-ratifies-africa-free-trade-area-agreement) in October 2025, but the instrument has yet to be deposited. South Sudan, according to a [CAMEPI study](https://camepi.org/the-african-continental-free-trade-areas-afcfta-implementation-in-cameroon-south-sudan-and-zambia/) published in January 2026, has still not deposited its own. Libya and Sudan are consumed by internal conflict. Benin, a participant in the ECOWAS Trade Liberalisation Scheme, remains the only coastal West African country not to have ratified, even though its economy lives largely off transit trade and re-exports to its neighbours.",
          "The Guided Trade Initiative served as a test bed. Launched in Accra in October 2022 with [around a hundred products](https://graphic.com.gh/news/general-news/afcfta-guided-trade-launched-96-products-set-for-trading.html) and a handful of pilot countries, it was gradually widened to 39 countries. According to the [report by Mahamadou Issoufou](https://www.tralac.org/documents/resources/cfta/7847-progress-report-on-the-afcfta-by-h-e-mahamadou-issoufou-former-president-of-the-republic-of-niger-and-leader-on-afcfta-february-2025.html), the AfCFTA Champion, presented to the February 2025 summit, it produced 2,850 certificates of origin before being wound up, its procedures to be folded into the agreement's ordinary operation. The figure reveals the nature of the exercise: a demonstration that the administrative chain works, not a volume of trade. In Balassa's terms, the AfCFTA is a free trade area under construction, not yet a free trade area in operation."
