          "A free trade area, unlike a customs union, lets each member set its own external tariff. Rules of origin are therefore needed to prevent a product imported from Asia through the lowest-tariff country from then circulating freely throughout the area. These rules determine what counts as “African”. By mid-2025 they had been agreed for 92.43% of tariff lines. The two remaining sectors, textiles and clothing and automobiles, are those where industrial policy is at stake. According to [Financial Afrik](https://www.financialafrik.com/en/2025/09/06/afcfta-rules-of-origin-facing-obstacles-in-the-automotive-and-textile-sectors/), West African cotton producers are at odds with textile exporters such as Egypt, Ethiopia and Morocco, and countries with nascent car industries, such as Morocco, South Africa and Rwanda, with countries that fear for their markets. The October 2025 deadline was missed: in December the [US Department of Commerce](https://www.trade.gov/market-intelligence/ghana-african-continental-free-trade-area-afcfta-december-2025-update) noted that both chapters remained open.",
          "Jacob Viner's distinction clarifies the dispute. A strict rule of origin, requiring for instance that fabric itself be spun and woven in Africa, protects African mills at the expense of garment makers that import cheaper Asian fabric. It encourages trade diversion in Viner's sense, since an efficient outside supplier is replaced by a costlier regional one simply because only the latter qualifies for the preference. A lenient rule favours trade creation and downstream competitiveness, but offers little to fibre producers. The disagreement is thus no technicality: it concerns how the gains are shared among countries sitting at different stages of the same value chain.",
          "The second piece of plumbing is financial. A Kenyan importer paying a Ghanaian supplier has until now usually had to convert into dollars and go through a correspondent bank outside the continent, with the delays and fees that entails. The Pan-African Payment and Settlement System (PAPSS), formally launched in January 2023 by Afreximbank and the African Union, allows settlement in local currencies with final clearing between central banks. When the Bank of Algeria joined in August 2025, [PAPSS](https://papss.com/?p=6973) reported 18 countries, more than 150 commercial banks and 14 payment switches connected. This is real progress, but the network still covers fewer than half the states parties, and the value of settled transactions is not published regularly, which makes it impossible to gauge actual use."
