          "Why do governments ratify quickly an agreement they implement slowly? Gene Grossman and Elhanan Helpman showed in 1995 that a free trade agreement wins domestic political support more easily the more it shields domestic producers from outside competition, that is, the more it diverts trade, and meets more opposition the more trade it creates. Sectoral exclusions then serve to make the agreement palatable. The AfCFTA fits this pattern: signing costs little and earns diplomatic capital, whereas domestication, customs reform and the liberalisation of sensitive products impose concentrated and visible losses on protected industries, informal intermediaries and public revenue.",
          "The fiscal constraint is real. In many African states, border duties remain a significant budget resource, easier to collect than income tax or domestic VAT. Liberalisation phased over five to thirteen years, depending on development level and product category, softens the shock, but it leaves administrations tempted to recoup through non-tariff levies what they lose in duties. The coexistence of eight recognised regional economic communities adds another layer: a country belonging to ECOWAS and WAEMU, or to SADC and COMESA, already manages several preferential regimes and several certificates of origin. The AfCFTA does not replace them; it sits on top of them, and traders choose the cheapest regime, which is often still the regional one.",
