          "In 1971 Raymond Vernon described what he called the “obsolescing bargain”: before the investment is made, the mining company holds the bargaining power, because the host state needs its capital and know-how; once the mine is built, the assets are sunk and the balance tips the other way, leaving the state free to rewrite the terms of the contract. Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger, joined since July 2024 in the Confederation of Sahel States (AES), have exploited this reversal more systematically than any other group of African countries since the nationalisations of the 1970s. They have done so just as the gold price climbed to unprecedented levels.",
          "On January 25, 2026, gold broke through $5,000 an ounce in New York for the first time, according to [Bernama](https://web11.bernama.com/en/news.php?id=2516776). At the end of January, Burkina Faso announced record output of more than 94 tonnes in 2025 and more than 776 billion CFA francs in budget revenue from gold, according to [Sika Finance](https://www.sikafinance.com/marches/burkina-faso-plus-de-776-milliards-fcfa-de-recettes-budgetaires-generees-par-l-or-en-2025_59459). In November 2025, Barrick agreed to pay Mali about $430 million to end a two-year dispute; in December, the Confederation launched its investment bank in Bamako. The question is therefore no longer whether the AES can capture more rent, but whether that capture can fund a lasting political project without drying up the investment that generates it."
