          "Both countries have gone beyond equity stakes. In 2024 Burkina Faso took over the Boungou and Wahgnion mines, which Endeavour Mining had sold to Lilium, and in June 2025 completed the transfer of two producing mines and three exploration permits to the Société de participation minière du Burkina (SOPAMIB), according to [Mining Weekly](https://www.miningweekly.com/article/burkina-faso-completes-nationalisation-of-five-gold-mining-assets-2025-06-12). In September 2025 President Ibrahim Traoré said he intended gradually to take control of the mines or to run them jointly. In Bamako, the state will hold 62% of the first national gold refinery, with a planned capacity of 200 tonnes a year, built with the Russian firm Yadran. The dispute with Barrick ended on November 24, 2025 with a settlement of 244 billion CFA francs, the return of operational control to the miner, the withdrawal of the ICSID arbitration and the release of four detained employees, according to [Reuters](https://www.kitco.com/news/off-the-wire/2025-11-25/canadas-barrick-pay-430-million-settle-mali-gold-mine-dispute).",
