          "The Democratic Republic of the Congo is the textbook case. It produced about 73% of the world's mined cobalt in 2025, according to the [USGS](https://pubs.usgs.gov/periodicals/mcs2026/mcs2026-cobalt.pdf), and Chinese companies held stakes in fifteen of the country's nineteen cobalt mines in 2023. CMOC alone accounted for more than 40% of global supply in 2024, and about two-thirds of world mine output belongs to Chinese companies according to Darton Commodities, cited by [Mining.com](https://www.mining.com/web/congo-suspends-cobalt-exports-for-four-months-amid-oversupply/). Oversupply had pushed the price below $10 a pound in early 2025, a level unseen in two decades barring a brief exception. Kinshasa responded on February 22, 2025 by suspending exports, extended twice and then replaced from October 16 by quotas: 18,125 tonnes for the rest of 2025, then 96,600 tonnes a year in 2026 and 2027, roughly what CMOC alone shipped in 2024, according to [Ecofin](https://www.ecofinagency.com/news/2209-48866-drc-to-resume-cobalt-export-in-1-month-but-set-quotas-putting-cmoc-under-pressure).",
