          "Zimbabwe is taking a parallel route for lithium. Raw ore exports have been banned since 2022, and concentrate exports will be banned from January 2027, the government announced in June 2025, according to [Reuters via Mining.com](https://www.mining.com/web/zimbabwe-to-ban-export-of-lithium-concentrates-from-2027/). The buyers are almost all Chinese: Sinomine, Zhejiang Huayou, Chengxin, Yahua and Canmax have invested more than $1 billion in the country since 2021, and two lithium sulfate plants, at Bikita and Arcadia, form the first stage of local processing. Guinea shows the other side of the relationship: the Simandou iron ore deposit, developed with Chinalco, China Baowu and the Hongqiao-led consortium, shipped its first cargo to China on December 2, 2025, according to [Caixin](https://www.caixinglobal.com/2025-12-03/simandou-iron-ore-mine-ships-first-cargo-to-china-102389356.html), with planned capacity of 120 million tonnes a year. Guinea gains a railway and a port; the question of forward linkages, a steel mill or a pelletizing plant, remains wide open."
